  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "The Integrated Public Alert & Warning System (IPAWS) is FEMA's national, internet-based infrastructure that allows federal, state, local, tribal, and territorial authorities to send urgent, life-saving alerts to the public. It simultaneously delivers alerts via wireless emergency alerts (cell phones), the Emergency Alert System (radio/TV), and NOAA weather radio. Key Functions of IPAWS: Rapid Alerts: Enables alerting authorities to send a single message that is distributed instantly to multiple communication channels. Targeted Messaging: Delivers geo-targeted warnings to specific geographic areas, ensuring relevant information reaches people at risk. Unified System: Provides a secure, internet-based interface for local authorities to send warnings, such as Amber Alerts or imminent threat alerts, directly to mobile devices and local broadcast channels. All-Hazards Capability: IPAWS supports various alert types, including weather emergencies, public health, and missing persons. How it Works:Authorized public safety officials (called Collaborative Operating Groups or COGs) utilize IPAWS to issue warnings. These messages are then pushed to cell phone carriers and media outlets, ensuring broad distribution of crucial safety information.",
